gm-method (a dependency-FILE-EDIT fix that 'was working' regresses for no apparent reason): when you fix a bug by EDITING a file inside a sibling/vendored dependency (not adding a new file) and then 'move the fix to the dep's source' + REMOVE the postinstall bridge, it regresses the instant a fresh `npm install` (or an older lockfile pin, or a stale published tarball) lands a version of that file WITHOUT the edit - the consumer silently runs the unpatched code again. Diagnose by GREPPING the INSTALLED node_modules copy for the fix's marker (a unique symbol the patch introduces, e.g. a program/uniform name) - if the count is low/zero the install predates the fix even if the sibling source + a published version number look fixed. FIX durably: keep a MARKER-GATED postinstall bridge - vendor the fixed file (or a targeted text-patch) and apply it ONLY when the installed copy LACKS the marker (so a future published version carrying it natively is not clobbered + the bridge no-ops). Do NOT remove the bridge on 'it's in the source now' - remove it only when the consumed PUBLISHED version provably carries the fix (grep the installed copy). General: published-version-number advancing != your specific commit is in the tarball the consumer resolved.